WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.139
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:04.139 --> 00:00:07.599
a HashMap to associate keys with values, replace

00:00:07.599 --> 00:00:10.988
a stored value, and retrieve information by its

00:00:10.988 --> 00:00:11.276
key.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:14.936
These associations let applications find

00:00:14.936 --> 00:00:17.867
quantities by product name or totals by

00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:21.098
category, giving later reports and business

00:00:21.098 --> 00:00:24.630
rules a consistent way to work with named data.

00:00:25.284 --> 00:00:28.367
A campus supply desk is checking its stock.

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:31.162
The names pen and tag identify kinds of

00:00:31.162 --> 00:00:34.388
supplies, and each stored whole number is the

00:00:34.388 --> 00:00:37.041
quantity of physical items available.

00:00:37.041 --> 00:00:40.052
A corrected count must replace the earlier

00:00:40.052 --> 00:00:42.059
quantity for that same kind.

00:00:42.713 --> 00:00:45.740
We'll store two quantities, correct one of them,

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.768
and print the current quantities plus the number

00:00:48.768 --> 00:00:49.903
of recorded kinds.

00:00:49.903 --> 00:00:52.867
Then you'll predict a second stock report using

00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:55.138
different supplies before we run it.

00:00:55.182 --> 00:00:58.082
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:58.082 --> 00:00:58.757
Main.java.

00:01:02.687 --> 00:01:05.892
We need the HashMap class for our stock record.

00:01:05.892 --> 00:01:08.552
We'll import it, then put the program's

00:01:08.552 --> 00:01:11.689
statements inside main, the starting method in

00:01:11.689 --> 00:01:12.712
the Main class.

00:01:21.061 --> 00:01:23.225
The program structure is ready.

00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:26.505
Our stock record needs text keys for the supply

00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:29.088
names and whole-number values for the

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:29.856
quantities.

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:33.136
Let's create an empty map with those two roles.

00:01:41.582 --> 00:01:44.534
String identifies the key type, and Integer

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:46.319
identifies the value type.

00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:49.614
Integer is the object form used to store a whole

00:01:49.614 --> 00:01:52.772
number in this map; the generic value position

00:01:52.772 --> 00:01:54.488
cannot use primitive int.

00:01:54.488 --> 00:01:57.578
The new expression creates the empty map, and

00:01:57.578 --> 00:01:58.882
stock refers to it.

00:01:58.893 --> 00:02:01.836
Now we can record four pens and two tags.

00:02:01.836 --> 00:02:05.281
Each put call associates its first argument, the

00:02:05.281 --> 00:02:08.439
key, with its second argument, the quantity.

00:02:08.439 --> 00:02:11.741
Java automatically boxes each primitive number

00:02:11.741 --> 00:02:14.468
into the Integer form this map stores.

00:02:20.403 --> 00:02:23.636
Those statements establish two associations.

00:02:23.636 --> 00:02:27.015
The desk then counts five pens, so the earlier

00:02:27.015 --> 00:02:29.514
pen quantity is no longer current.

00:02:29.514 --> 00:02:33.040
We'll put the corrected value under the existing

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:33.628
pen key.

00:02:37.006 --> 00:02:40.195
That put replaces four with five; it does not

00:02:40.195 --> 00:02:43.243
add five to four or create another pen key.

00:02:43.243 --> 00:02:45.794
The tag association stays unchanged.

00:02:45.794 --> 00:02:48.983
To build the report, we'll use get to look up

00:02:48.983 --> 00:02:52.031
each quantity by name and print it beside a

00:02:52.031 --> 00:02:52.456
label.

00:03:03.089 --> 00:03:06.164
Each lookup supplies the Integer value currently

00:03:06.164 --> 00:03:07.701
associated with its key.

00:03:07.701 --> 00:03:10.775
Joining that result to a text label displays the

00:03:10.775 --> 00:03:13.786
number, so readers see the quantity rather than

00:03:13.786 --> 00:03:16.220
needing to understand its stored type.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.910
Both keys were inserted earlier; a missing

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.856
lookup would instead return null, which is not

00:03:21.856 --> 00:03:22.689
numeric zero.

00:03:22.706 --> 00:03:26.187
The report also needs the number of supply kinds

00:03:26.187 --> 00:03:26.913
on record.

00:03:26.913 --> 00:03:30.394
Size counts the map's distinct keys, not the sum

00:03:30.394 --> 00:03:31.700
of its quantities.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.601
Let's add that final line and finish the

00:03:34.601 --> 00:03:35.181
program.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.638
The complete program now corrects one quantity

00:03:43.638 --> 00:03:46.933
and asks two different questions: how many items

00:03:46.933 --> 00:03:50.091
of each kind are available, and how many kinds

00:03:50.091 --> 00:03:50.984
are recorded.

00:03:50.984 --> 00:03:53.730
Let's click Run and check those results.

00:03:58.772 --> 00:04:02.052
Pens is five because the later put replaced the

00:04:02.052 --> 00:04:02.959
earlier four.

00:04:02.959 --> 00:04:04.006
Tags stays two.

00:04:04.006 --> 00:04:07.076
Types is two because pen and tag are the two

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:08.053
distinct keys.

00:04:08.053 --> 00:04:11.332
The current quantities total seven items, while

00:04:11.332 --> 00:04:12.868
size counts two kinds.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:16.008
Our explicit print statements determine which

00:04:16.008 --> 00:04:17.822
report line appears first.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.384
The map gives each name a current value.

00:04:21.384 --> 00:04:24.701
A replacement changes that association without

00:04:24.701 --> 00:04:26.359
changing the key count.

00:04:26.359 --> 00:04:29.532
We did not need list positions or a promised

00:04:29.532 --> 00:04:32.632
HashMap traversal order to find these named

00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:33.425
quantities.

00:04:33.466 --> 00:04:36.519
Let's apply the same rules to the desk's map and

00:04:36.519 --> 00:04:37.346
kit supplies.

00:04:37.346 --> 00:04:40.209
We'll replace the worked program with a fresh

00:04:40.209 --> 00:04:43.198
stock record, enter its initial quantities, and

00:04:43.198 --> 00:04:44.789
correct the map quantity.

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:47.587
Keep your prediction for the complete report

00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:49.941
until all its statements are visible.

00:05:12.446 --> 00:05:15.882
The two starting associations and the correction

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:16.741
are entered.

00:05:16.741 --> 00:05:19.533
Now we'll add the named lookups and the

00:05:19.533 --> 00:05:22.826
key-count report, using the same operations as

00:05:22.826 --> 00:05:23.971
the worked case.

00:05:39.223 --> 00:05:42.400
Before running the completed program, predict

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.588
all three labeled output lines.

00:05:44.588 --> 00:05:47.977
Track the current value for each key after every

00:05:47.977 --> 00:05:50.377
put, then decide what size counts.

00:05:50.377 --> 00:05:52.848
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:06:00.912 --> 00:06:04.005
Let's click Run to compare the program's report

00:06:04.005 --> 00:06:05.387
with your prediction.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:13.514
Maps is three because the second put for map

00:06:13.514 --> 00:06:16.018
replaced its earlier quantity of two.

00:06:16.018 --> 00:06:17.034
Kits stays one.

00:06:17.034 --> 00:06:20.079
Types is two because the record still has the

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:21.636
same two distinct keys.

00:06:21.636 --> 00:06:24.411
The quantities add to four, but that is a

00:06:24.411 --> 00:06:27.186
different total from the number of kinds.

00:06:27.901 --> 00:06:30.622
The desk now has a report of its corrected

00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:31.011
stock.

00:06:31.011 --> 00:06:33.991
Keys identify the supplies, put maintains each

00:06:33.991 --> 00:06:36.907
current value, and get retrieves those values

00:06:36.907 --> 00:06:37.619
for output.

00:06:37.619 --> 00:06:40.470
Distinguishing quantities from the key count

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.515
makes the report meaningful and prepares you to

00:06:43.515 --> 00:06:46.301
build the frequency tables in the notebook.

00:06:47.042 --> 00:06:50.175
Suppose a recount changes a recorded supply

00:06:50.175 --> 00:06:51.414
quantity to zero.

00:06:51.414 --> 00:06:54.547
Would that put remove the key or change the

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:56.368
number of recorded kinds?

00:06:56.368 --> 00:06:59.793
Explain how you would distinguish that recorded

00:06:59.793 --> 00:07:03.217
zero from a supply name that was never entered.
